Ways the change could fail a criterion (what each rating is measured against):

  smallest-edit-that-makes-defect-true
    [other] smallest-edit-that-makes-defect-true/descriptor-can-return-non-minimal-edit
      The descriptor stage can accept any contiguous replacement that makes the defect true, but nothing in the delivered code checks that the chosen edit is actually the smallest one, so a larger-than-necessary mutant could be built.
      src/acceptance/mutation/descriptor.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/validity.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/runner.py#0
    [not_wired] smallest-edit-that-makes-defect-true/execution-disabled-by-default
      The execution tier is opt-in and `run_review` skips it entirely when `execution` is `None` or `enabled` is false, so the review can still proceed without ever building a mutant edit at all.
      src/acceptance/mutation/settings.py#0
      src/acceptance/pipeline.py#2
      src/acceptance/pipeline.py#4
    [missing_case] smallest-edit-that-makes-defect-true/declined-defects-never-mutated
      When `build_descriptors` returns `None` for a defect, `run_mutations` records it as `not_mutable` instead of constructing any edit, so defects that need a different representation than one contiguous span are not turned into a mutant at all.
      src/acceptance/mutation/descriptor.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/runner.py#0
    [scope_too_narrow] smallest-edit-that-makes-defect-true/invalidity-checks-can-reject-valid-smallest-edit
      The validity gate rejects any descriptor whose replacement spans more than `DEFAULT_MAX_EDIT_LINES`, even if that is the smallest edit that makes the defect true, so some plausible defects are excluded from mutation on size alone.
      src/acceptance/mutation/validity.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/settings.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/runner.py#0

  run-candidate-tests-on-altered-copy
    [not_wired] run-candidate-tests-on-altered-copy/execution-tier-never-invoked
      The execution tier is only entered when `execution` is provided and `enabled` is true, so the candidate tests are never run on the altered copy when the new option is omitted or left off.
      src/acceptance/pipeline.py#2
      src/acceptance/pipeline.py#3
      src/acceptance/pipeline.py#4
      src/acceptance/cli.py#1
      src/acceptance/cli.py#2
      src/acceptance/cli.py#4
    [scope_too_narrow] run-candidate-tests-on-altered-copy/baseline-halt-skips-mutation-run
      A failing candidate test at head halts the review before any mutant is created or executed, so the altered-copy test run does not happen for that class of inputs unless the override is set.
      src/acceptance/mutation/baseline.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/settings.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/runner.py#0
      src/acceptance/pipeline.py#2
      src/acceptance/cli.py#3
      src/acceptance/cli.py#4
    [other] run-candidate-tests-on-altered-copy/mutated-copy-not-used-for-test-run
      The mutation runner could still end up calling `run_tests` on the original project root instead of the temporary mutated workspace, because the test execution path is wired separately from the copy-and-apply step and the code only proves the copy is created, not that the runner always uses it.
      src/acceptance/mutation/injection.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/runner.py#0

  test-fails-discriminate-defect
    [other] test-fails-discriminate-defect/failing-tests-not-marked-as-killing
      When a mutant run produces a failing candidate test, the code records the attempt as `KILLED` and stores the test in `killing_tests`, but there is no explicit path shown that turns that into the review-level discrimination record for the named defect; if the downstream verdict assembly or report wiring misses it, a failing test would not be shown as discriminating.
      src/acceptance/mutation/runner.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/verdicts.py#0
      src/acceptance/pipeline.py#4
      src/acceptance/report.py#1
    [missing_case] test-fails-discriminate-defect/baseline-halt-prevents-discrimination
      If the control run finds a candidate test already failing at head and `--allow-failing-tests` is not set, the pipeline raises `ReviewHalted` and returns exit 2 instead of recording any discrimination for that defect, so failing tests are not shown as discriminating in this case.
      src/acceptance/mutation/baseline.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/settings.py#0
      src/acceptance/cli.py#4
      src/acceptance/pipeline.py#2

  candidate-tests-not-discriminate-when-none-fail
    [missing_case] candidate-tests-not-discriminate-when-none-fail/no-discrimination-report-when-execution-finds-no-kills
      When execution runs and every candidate test passes against every injected mutant, the pipeline still only appends the executed verdicts and derived support; there is no explicit finding or report text that says the candidate tests do not discriminate for the defect in that no-fail case.
      src/acceptance/pipeline.py#4
      src/acceptance/pipeline.py#5
      src/acceptance/report.py#0
      src/acceptance/report.py#1
    [scope_too_narrow] candidate-tests-not-discriminate-when-none-fail/survival-with-partially-observed-run-treated-as-no-discrimination
